VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: F1
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Scope: internal/services/storage/migration.go (+ migration_cache_test.go), attempt 2,
against ruling 2026-08-22d (confidentiality, not staleness).

What I attacked and what held up:

1. Plaintext reachability after delete(s.cache, path): traced cacheEntry.data —
   ReadFileContext always returns a defensive copy (storage.go:306-309,
   342-344), never the entry's own backing array, so no external caller can
   hold an alias into the map's storage. After invalidateCache's delete, the
   evicted cacheEntry and its []byte are unreachable from Storage and
   collectable. No aliasing/backup/snapshot path found that retains it
   (backup's buildZip reads raw on-disk bytes via os.ReadFile directly,
   never through s.cache, so it can't be a residency vector for this map).

2. Bypass paths: grepped the whole package (and auth.go) for
   os.WriteFile/os.Rename/os.Remove. Only three helpers mutate a
   cache-eligible data path outside migration.go: writeFileLocked,
   CreateExclusive, removeLocked — all pre-existing, all already bracket
   invalidateCache on both sides (unrelated to this task). migration.go's
   three helpers are the only other data-file mutators, and all three now
   bracket correctly per my read of the shipped file.

3. Verify/marker/config exclusion: grepped every reference to verifyFile,
   markerFile, configFile across the entire repo (not just this package).
   All three are always read via raw os.ReadFile/os.Stat, never via
   s.ReadFile/s.ReadFileContext. The "never read through cache" argument for
   excluding them holds under a full-repo search, not just a package-local
   one.

4. Concurrency: encryptFileWithRecipient/decryptFileWithIdentity/
   rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity are unexported; grepped all production
   callers — reachable only from EnableEncryptionWithProvider/
   DisableEncryption, both of which hold s.mu.Lock() for the entire call.
   Tests call the unexported helpers directly without the lock, but that's
   single-threaded test-only access, not a production interleaving.

5. Regression: ran the full package suite against the merged tree unmodified
   — all Cache/Rollback/Migration tests plus the full suite pass (`go test
   ./internal/services/storage/...` → ok, 40s). Ordinary reads still cache
   (TestReadCachesUnmigratedFile passes).

The defect (test-coverage gap, not a live leak):

The report's "mutation-confirmed" claim is demonstrated for exactly one of
three structurally identical call sites. I reproduced the same class of bug
(invalidateCache moved from before the fallible transform to after it — the
exact wt-alt mistake the report calls out) in the other two helpers, on a
throwaway copy under /tmp, and re-ran the full package suite each time:

  a) decryptFileWithIdentity: moved its invalidateCache from before
     decryptData to immediately after it (mirrors the rejected wt-alt
     placement). `go test ./internal/services/storage/...` → ok, no
     failures. TestDecryptFileWithIdentityCorruptData exercises the decrypt
     failure but never populates the cache first, so it can't observe
     residency; no test does.

  b) rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity: moved its invalidateCache from before
     decryptData to after it, i.e. removed all protection on the
     decrypt-failure branch (the `continue` on decryptData err). `go test
     ./internal/services/storage/...` → ok, no failures.
     TestRollbackEncryptionWithBadDecrypt exercises exactly this branch
     (wrong identity -> decrypt fails -> continue) but only asserts the file
     stays encrypted on disk; it never populates the cache beforehand, so it
     cannot see a stranded plaintext entry. TestRollbackEncryptionEvictsCache
     dPlaintext populates the cache first but only exercises the
     decrypt-succeeds path, so it doesn't cover this branch either.

Both mutations reintroduce, in the two helpers not covered by
TestEncryptFailureDoesNotLeavePlaintextCached, precisely the failure-path
residency bug that is this task's entire subject and that the Tier-3 report
identifies as "the exact condition this task exists to remove" — with zero
detection anywhere in the suite. Rollback is the more serious of the two
gaps: the report's own framing (and this brief's "angles" list) singles out
rollback as "already the failure path," yet it is the one call site with the
weakest test defense of all three.

Direction of the defect: this is not a live confidentiality breach in the
shipped code today — I read the actual migration.go and both
decryptFileWithIdentity and rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity already place
invalidateCache correctly (before the transform). The gap is that this
correct placement is unverified for 2 of the 3 helpers: a future edit
reproducing the exact wt-alt mistake in either helper would reintroduce
plaintext-residency-on-failure silently, with the full suite still green.
Given the constitution's explicit warning about "a fix applied at one call
site but not its twin," and that the task's own framing treats rollback as
the highest-value failure path to defend, I'm treating this as a real
defect in the attempt rather than an out-of-scope nice-to-have.

What I did not find: no live plaintext-residency defect, no bypass of the
three data-file mutators, no incorrect verify/marker/config exclusion, no
concurrency hole in the production call graph, no regression in ordinary
caching behavior.
